            "text": "{\n  \"judgments\": [\n    {\n      \"proposal_id\": \"P0000\",\n      \"source_refs\": [\n        \"F000:F001\",\n        \"F010\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"Subsection (d)(1) establishes an agency's obligation to grant an individual access to their records and to permit an accompanying person upon request, with discretionary authority to request written authorization for that accompanying person. The restriction concerning records compiled in reasonable anticipation of a civil action or proceeding is an independent, section-wide rule established in subsection (d)(5) and already captured as a separate claim. Incorporating this general exclusion into the specific requirement of subsection (d)(1) blurs distinct statutory provisions and does not repair a genuine defect in the baseline claim.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"unsupported\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"proposal_id\": \"P0001\",\n      \"source_refs\": [\n        \"F000:F001\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"The source text in subsection (d)(1) states that 'the agency may require the individual to furnish a written statement authorizing discussion of that individual’s record in the accompanying person’s presence.' It does not impose a mandatory duty ('must require'), nor does it mandate authorization statements from unaccompanied individuals. Modifying the summary to assert that the agency must require every individual, accompanied or unaccompanied, to provide written authorization contradicts both the discretionary modal force ('may') and the substantive scope of the statutory exception.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"unsupported\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
